// PLAYTEST: is a deeper kit worth a weaker one? An Invoker holds six
// routine slots at level 5 where everyone else holds four, and every
// routine it runs is authored 15% up on Buff and Debuff against a
// specialist's 30%.
//
// The sixth of seven class files — see `player-class-striker.ron` for the
// shared argument: why the pack is this pack, why the pool is unspent, and
// why the headless bin reports the same numbers for every class.
//
//     FERAL_DEV_ARENA=1 cargo run  ->  [R] Arena  ->  [L] player-class-invoker
//
// **This is the one class file where the headless floor is not the only
// thing the bin cannot see.** `PartyPlan::AllAttack` invokes nothing, so it
// misses the spread like it does for the other seven — but it also never
// opens the routine menu, which is the entire feature. Six rows against four
// is a thing you can only look at.
//
// What to watch:
//   - Open the routine list. Are the two extra slots *full*? An Invoker
//     whose species unlocks and starter do not reach six is carrying two
//     empty rows, and the class is worth nothing until much later than the
//     screen implies. That is the first thing to falsify.
//   - `hyperthread` at 1.15 against a Bastion's 1.3 on the same routine: is
//     a 15-point gap in authored buff power visible across a fight, or does
//     breadth cost nothing you can feel?
//   - Does having more slots change what you *do*, or just what you could
//     do? If the fight is won by invoking the same one routine every time,
//     the slots are a menu and not a class.
//   - The Invoker damps Damage at 0.85 and this kit has no damage routine,
//     so the trade is invisible here by construction — the same hole every
//     other class file in this set has, and the same finding if the class
//     reads as strictly better.
(
    player: Fresh(level: 5, zone: 1),
    character: (
        class: Some(Invoker),
        stats: (0, 0, 0, 0),
        routine: Some("hyperthread"),
    ),
    party: [
        (species: "glitch", level: 5),
        (species: "glitch", level: 5),
    ],
    opponents: [
        (species: "sub_process", count: 5),
        (species: "sub_process", count: 5),
    ],
    reps: 1,
    seed: 2,
)
